A||N||6||fropuff||Is there an easier way?||||10||
Z||000000||fropuff||06-06-2000||06:47 PM||||Hey, I was wondering if there is an easier way to wet sand my GT.  I am getting ready for a paint job, and I'm not even getting anywhere.  I sand till it's dull.  Then I see one spot I missed.  Then I go through the primer and into the fiberglass! (which I'm not supposed to do.)  I'm having a pretty tough time.  What can I do to make it easier for myself? (Besides hiring someone to do it.  What would they charge anyway?)<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 06-06-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||Uaana||06-06-2000||08:19 PM||||O.K. <BR>What kind of damage are you trying to remove? checking? or sunfade? If it is checking you will have to take it almost down to the plastic to really take it out, if it is sunfade try using a lighter grade of paper (what are you using now?). Are you hand sanding or using a power sander/buffer?||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||06-06-2000||08:52 PM||||I'm hand sanding with 1000 grit.  The guy told me either 1000 or 1200.  I picked 1000. I tried to sand it with a palm sander, but it seemed to squirrly for me to trust myself.  Am I using too high of a grade?  Should I try the palm sander on the flat surfaces?  I don't know what to do.  I was sanding for an hour straight and all I got done was about a square foot. <P>BTW:  I had some clear coat damage.  I sanded that out, but now I have to sand to get all the gloss (clearcoat) out of the paint for it to stick.  Any other suggestions?  Thanks a lot.||||reg||10
Z||000003||Sage||06-06-2000||10:07 PM||||If you are prepping for a paint job, use #400 or #600 on a sponge or flexible block and stay off the corners.  #1000 is usually used to sand out bad orange peel on a fresh paint job.  Use the block on large flat areas, the sponge on round or curved surfaces.  Are you going to prime/seal before you paint? You should.  Make sure everything is completly and thoroughly DRY before you put anything on it.  If there is any water(especially on the fiberglass panels if you went through to the glass)it will stay in there and when the sun hits your new paint job, the water will boil and lift the finish.  I mean ANY water!  Wait till it is "bone dry"!  Just my .002<P>Good luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000004||fropuff||06-06-2000||11:29 PM||||No, the guy is not going to prime before he paints it.  I'll tell you what he is using.. DCU 2042 (I have no idea what it is, but it's a PPG product). He's using PPG plastic primer and sealer, but that's just for my crappy front fascia.  He might also use them for the car. I don't know.  He told me a quart of each; Could he be doing this for the car???  Finally, he asked for a DT 877; he said this is a reducer.  Don't ask me what this means.  I have no idea.  As you can tell, I'm not an expert when it comes to what's going on.  Oh yeah, if I'm not going to strip everything, should I use a grit around 800 or something like that?  I don't want to screw up what he told me.  He seems to know a lot about this subject.  Thanks a lot.||||reg||10
Z||000005||fieroshop||06-07-2000||02:20 AM||||Sounds like your guy knows what to do but he is getting rather skimpy on the paint. You should sand everything thourghly and then use an ecthing primer, then spray with a flexible primer and then sand lightly with four hundred grit until you get most of the orange peel and then sand smooth with 1000 wet. After that you need to have the base coat sprayed  and then comes the final stage of clear. After the car has dried properly then you color sand with 1000 wet and finally 1500 wet and then buff out the paint and then you are done except for the detailing and cleaning all the stuff of your car. Sound complicated? Well it is and my suggestion is to get a professional to do it for you. You will save yourself a lot of grief. Asking how I know all this? Well I'll tell you. I am a professional and we do paint at my shop, besides all the other work. Our jobs come out so good that you can take the car to a show and it will look better then most cars out there.<BR>Oh I forgot the most important part. You start out by sanding with 240 grit then switch to 400 grit and that is before the primers. Also all the primers need to have flex agent in them or the paint will crack unless it is a flexible primer.<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 06-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sage||06-07-2000||08:23 AM||||Good show George! FROPUFF:  Listen to this man, he knows what he is talking about! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
